parts.text:
The compilation ran successfully and produced 58 pages of output! There are some errors though:

1. `! Extra }, or forgotten $` - This is related to the `\placeholderfig` command. The issue is with the `\textbf` inside `\framebox`. The `\framebox` command puts its content in text mode, but there might be an issue with the way I'm handling it. Actually, looking at the error more carefully, it's complaining about the closing `}` after the `\textit`. 

The issue is that `\framebox[\textwidth]{...}` expects text content, and within it I'm using `\centering` and `\vspace`. Let me look at my `\placeholderfig` definition more carefully:

```latex
\newcommand{\placeholderfig}[2]{%
  \begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \framebox[\textwidth]{%
      \parbox{0.9\textwidth}{\centering\vspace{2cm}\textbf{#1}\\\textit{(Placeholder -- insert actual diagram)}\vspace{2cm}}%
    }
    \caption{#2}
    \label{fig:#1}
  \end{figure}
}
```

The problem might be that `\parbox` doesn't like `\\` in certain contexts, or it could be that `\centering` is already applied by the figure environment and then applied again inside the parbox. Let me simplify the placeholder command.

Actually, the issue is that `\framebox` expects its content to be in LR (left-to-right) mode, and `\parbox` is allowed there. The `\\` inside the parbox should work in `\centering` mode. But the error message says something about `$` and `\egroup` on line 9 of appendix.tex which is the admin tokens placeholder.

Let me trace through: the `\placeholderfig{screenshot_admin_tokens}{Screenshot showing token management via the admin tool}` call on line 9 of appendix.tex

The `#1` is `screenshot_admin_tokens` and `#2` is `Screenshot showing token management via the admin tool`. The `--` in the caption might be causing issues. Actually `--` in LaTeX makes an en-dash, so that should be fine.

Wait, the error is about `show` after `token management via the admin tool`. The text contains `--` (two hyphens). In LaTeX, `--` is an en-dash, which is fine. But maybe the issue is with the `\textbf{#1}` where #1 contains underscores like `screenshot_admin_tokens`. Underscores are special characters in LaTeX and need to be escaped! That's the issue!

In the `\placeholderfig` command, #1 is used in `\textbf{#1}` and the underscore in `screenshot_admin_tokens` is causing a math-mode error because `_` is only allowed in math mode.

I need to either:
1. Use `\texttt` for the first argument (which handles underscores)
2. Or use `\textunderscore` 
3. Or use `\detokenize`
4. Or use `\protect\_` type escaping

The simplest fix is to use `\texttt` which automatically handles underscores, or use `\detokenize`. Actually, the cleanest fix is to just not use underscores in the placeholder labels. But the user might use underscores in their diagram filenames. Let me use `\texttt{#1}` or `\detokenize{#1}`.

Actually, the simplest fix is to change the placeholder command to use `\detokenize` for the text argument:

```latex
\newcommand{\placeholderfig}[2]{%
  \begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \framebox[\textwidth]{%
      \parbox{0.9\textwidth}{\centering\vspace{2cm}\textbf{\detokenize{#1}}\\\textit{(Placeholder -- insert actual diagram)}\vspace{2cm}}%
    }
    \caption{#2}
    \label{fig:#1}
  \end{figure}
}
```

Wait, but `\label{fig:#1}` will also have the underscore issue. Actually, no - `\label` with underscores... let me check. The `\label{fig:screenshot_admin_tokens}` should work because `\label` just stores the string; it doesn't typeset it. Underscores in labels are actually allowed in some LaTeX implementations.

Actually, the standard `\label` command DOES allow underscores because it just stores the text as a key. The `_` character is only problematic in text mode when typesetting. So the issue is specifically in `\textbf{#1}` where #1 contains `_`.

The fix is simple - use `\detokenize{#1}` for the text display:

Actually wait, `\detokenize` might also have issues. Let me use a different approach. I'll just use `\texttt` which naturally handles underscores, or better yet, I'll just use `\texttt` since it's a filename/identifier anyway.

Or even simpler: just don't use underscores in the figure labels (change the pattern to use hyphens).

Actually, the cleanest fix is to define the command to use `\detokenize`:

```latex
\newcommand{\placeholderfig}[2]{%
  \begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \framebox[\textwidth]{%
      \parbox{0.9\textwidth}{\centering\vspace{2cm}\textbf{\detokenize{#1}}\\\textit{(Placeholder -- insert actual diagram)}\vspace{2cm}}%
    }
    \caption{#2}
    \label{fig:#1}
  \end{figure}
}
```

Hmm, `\detokenize` will also detokenize spaces and other characters, which changes appearance but is better than an error. 

Actually, looking at the error more carefully - the error is about `\\` in the parbox. `\\` is not allowed in the `\centering` environment inside a `\parbox` in LR mode. The `\parbox` is inside `\framebox` which creates an LR box. In an LR box, `\\` is not valid.

Let me redesign the placeholder command to avoid these issues. Instead of using `\framebox` and `\parbox`, I can use `\makebox` or a `\fbox` with a `\minipage`:

```latex
\newcommand{\placeholderfig}[2]{%
  \begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \fbox{%
      \begin{minipage}{0.9\textwidth}
        \centering\vspace{2cm}
        \textbf{#1}\\
        \textit{(Placeholder -- insert actual diagram)}
        \vspace{2cm}
      \end{minipage}%
    }
    \caption{#2}
    \label{fig:#1}
  \end{figure}
}
```

The `minipage` environment properly supports `\\`. And `\fbox` is the same as `\framebox` essentially. Let me use this approach.

Also for the underscore issue: within a `minipage` (which is in paragraph mode), `_` needs to be escaped. But since the #1 argument comes from user input with underscores, I should still use `\detokenize` or escape it.

Actually, the simplest solution is to not have underscores in the figure labels in the report itself. I'll change the calls to use spaces or hyphens instead:

Actually wait, let me just think about this differently. The figure labels are just identifiers. I could just change them to use hyphens:

```
\placeholderfig{screenshot-scheduler-startup}{...}
```

But the `\label{fig:...}` also needs to work. In LaTeX, `\label{fig:screenshot-scheduler-startup}` works fine. But users might want to use underscores.

Let me just fix the command to handle underscores properly. The cleanest fix is:

```latex
\newcommand{\placeholderfig}[2]{%
  \begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \fbox{%
      \begin{minipage}{0.9\textwidth}
        \centering\vspace{2cm}
        \textbf{\detokenize{#1}}\\
        \textit{(Placeholder --- insert actual diagram)}
        \vspace{2cm}
      \end{minipage}%
    }
    \caption{#2}
    \label{fig:#1}
  \end{figure}
}
```

`\detokenize` will convert `_` to `\_` effectively. Let me try this.

Actually, I just realized that `\detokenize` will expand `#1` and detokenize each token. For `screenshot_admin_tokens`, it will produce `screenshot_admin_tokens` with the underscores properly shown. That's actually fine.

But wait, there's still the `\\` issue. In a `minipage` with `\centering`, `\\` should work fine.

Let me also make sure `---` (em-dash) works. Actually, that's the correct LaTeX for an em dash. In the original I had `--` which is en-dash. Let me make it `---` for proper typography.

Let me fix this in the main report.tex.

